2️⃣ Collection Framework Overview

Understand this picture clearly:

Iterable
   |
Collection
   ├── List
   ├── Set
   └── Queue
Map (separate hierarchy)


Know:

Why Collections are needed

Difference between Collection and Map




📌 LIST Interface (Ordered, Allows Duplicates)
3️⃣ ArrayList

Learn:

Dynamic array concept

Methods: add, get, set, remove, size

Time complexity

ArrayList<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>();
list.add(10);
list.add(20);
System.out.println(list.get(0));




4️⃣ LinkedList

Learn:

Singly & Doubly linked list logic

When to use LinkedList vs ArrayList

LinkedList<String> names = new LinkedList<>();
names.add("Athi");
names.addFirst("Java");




📌 SET Interface (No Duplicates)
5️⃣ HashSet

Uses hashing

No order

Fast operations

Set<Integer> set = new HashSet<>();
set.add(10);
set.add(10); // ignored




6️⃣ LinkedHashSet

Maintains insertion order



7️⃣ TreeSet

Sorted order

Uses Red-Black Tree



📌 QUEUE Interface (FIFO)
8️⃣ PriorityQueue

Min-heap by default

Used in scheduling

PriorityQueue<Integer> pq = new PriorityQueue<>();
pq.add(30);
pq.add(10);
System.out.println(pq.poll()); // 10



📌 MAP Interface (Key-Value Pair)
9️⃣ HashMap ⭐ (Very Important)

No duplicate keys

Fast lookup

HashMap<Integer, String> map = new HashMap<>();
map.put(1, "Athi");
System.out.println(map.get(1));



🔟 LinkedHashMap

Maintains insertion order

1️⃣1️⃣ TreeMap

Sorted keys

